This file is a help2man include.  A [section] block goes in at the start of
that section and a [=section] block replaces it, while a /pattern/ block goes
in after the paragraph matching the pattern.  The note at the foot of this
file is attached to a pattern so that it lands at the end of COPYRIGHT, after
the copyright help2man makes out of --version, rather than ahead of it.
Should help2man's wording ever change, that note goes missing rather than
turning up in the wrong place.

[NAME]
buckle\-x11 \- Nostalgia bucklespring keyboard sound, X11 flavour

[=DESCRIPTION]
Buckle emulates the sound of an old IBM Model-M space saver
bucklespring keyboard while typing within an X session.
.PP
It runs as a background process and plays back the sound of each key
pressed and released on the keyboard, just as if using an IBM Model-M.
The sound of each key has carefully been sampled, and is played back
while simulating the proper distance and direction for a realistic 3D
sound palette of pure nostalgic bliss.
.PP
This flavour grabs key events through X11, and therefore only reports keys
typed within an X session.  For Wayland compositors and for the console, see
.BR buckle\-libinput (1).
.PP
The mouse clicks too: the buttons, and the scroll wheel, one click per detent.
Use
.B \-\-no\-click
to silence it, or to silence only some of it.
.PP
A key held down long enough to repeat clicks for every repeat.  The X server
does not repeat the modifier keys, so holding shift stays quiet by itself.

Where it was built with the system tray, and there is a tray to put it in, an
icon appears there.  Its menu holds the two things worth one click, pausing
and quitting, and an entry that opens the preferences window; rolling the
wheel over the icon changes the volume without opening the menu at all.
.B \-\-no\-tray
leaves the icon out.  It is a StatusNotifierItem, which KDE Plasma shows as it
is and GNOME Shell shows through an extension; where nothing is listening
there is simply no icon and nothing else changes.
.PP
The volume, the clip set, the audio device and the headphone filter are in
that window, and not in the menu: a menu on a tray icon travels over dbusmenu,
which carries labels and check marks rather than widgets, so a volume there
can only be a ladder of radio items and a device list has to be rebuilt as a
submenu whenever it changes.  It also means a desktop with nowhere to put the
icon still has all of it.  The running
.B buckle\-x11
puts the window up when it is asked:
.B \-\-preferences
asks over the session bus and exits; that second invocation plays nothing and
holds no sound device of its own.  The desktop file carries the same thing as
an action, named
.IR "Bucklespring preferences" ,
and
.B SIGUSR1
does it too, for a keyboard shortcut with no dbus tooling in it.  Closing the
window leaves the clicks running.

Each key is played at a position of its own, left to right as it sits on the
board, which
.B \-\-stereo\-width
scales.  On headphones
.B \-\-hrtf
runs that through OpenAL's head related transfer functions, which is the
difference between a key sounding placed somewhere around you and merely
panned between your ears.  OpenAL left to itself does this only when it
believes it is talking to headphones, and
.B \-\-hrtf=auto
is that default; the preferences window offers the same three choices while
running.

The samples live one set to a directory under
.IR /usr/share/bucklespring/clips ,
the Model M recordings the program began with being the set called
.IR bucklespring .
.B \-\-list\-clips
says which are installed and
.B \-\-clips
picks one; the preferences window lists them too and switches between them as
it runs, dropping the samples of the old set so that the new ones load as you
type.
.B \-p
still names a directory of wav files directly, for a set kept somewhere of
its own.  A set that does not cover every key wants
.B \-f
alongside it.
.PP
Clips are read with
.BR libsndfile (3),
so a set may be flac, wav, ogg or opus, the first of those found for a key
winning in that order.  The set shipped with the program is flac, lossless
and a little under half the size of the same samples as wav; ogg and opus are
smaller again at the cost of being lossy.  Anything with more than one channel
is mixed down to mono, OpenAL positioning only single channel buffers.

[SEE ALSO]
.BR buckle\-x11.service (8),
.BR buckle\-libinput (1),
.BR update\-alternatives (1)

